# WebGL: No Threads

Our builds target WebGL, which runs **single-threaded**. C# threading APIs either throw at runtime, deadlock, or silently degrade to main-thread execution. Write all code main-thread-only.

## `Task.Delay` never completes on WebGL

`Task.Delay` is backed by `System.Threading.Timer`. WebGL has no timer thread, so the continuation after `await Task.Delay(...)` is **never scheduled** — the game hangs forever on whatever screen was showing when the delay started.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — hangs forever on WebGL (offline boot, reconnect backoff, AI pacing, etc.)
await Task.Delay(250);
await Task.Delay(500, token);

// ✅ GOOD — player-loop timer; works on WebGL
await Awaitable.WaitForSecondsAsync(0.25f, destroyCancellationToken);
await MainThreadDelay.WaitMillisecondsAsync(500, token);
```

Use `MainThreadDelay` (`MonkeyTilt.Foundation`, namespace `MonkeyTilt.Shared`) or `Awaitable.WaitForSecondsAsync` directly. After every `await` in a MonoBehaviour, guard `if (this == null) return`.

`WebGlThreadingApiGuard` (build preprocess, `MonkeyTilt.Shared.Guards.Editor`) fails WebGL builds when runtime code matches banned threading APIs below.

## Banned APIs (specific — not whole namespaces)

`using System.Threading.Tasks` for `Task`-returning async methods is fine. `using System.Threading` for `CancellationToken` is fine. Ban these **calls**:

| Banned | Why |
|--------|-----|
| `Task.Delay` | Timer never fires on WebGL |
| `Thread.Sleep` | Blocks the only thread |
| `Task.Wait()` / `.Result` / `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` | Deadlock — no thread to complete the task |
| `Task.Run` / `Task.Factory.StartNew` | No thread pool |
| `new Thread(...)` / `ThreadPool.QueueUserWorkItem` | No threads |
| `Parallel.For` / `Parallel.ForEach` / PLINQ | No parallelism |
| `new System.Threading.Timer` / `new System.Timers.Timer` | No timer thread |
| `lock` / `Monitor` / `Mutex` / `SemaphoreSlim` expecting concurrency | Dead weight; signals threaded design |

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — deadlocks on WebGL
var data = FetchAsync().Result;
Task.Run(() => BuildGrid());

// ✅ GOOD — await on the main thread
var data = await FetchAsync();

// ✅ GOOD — heavy work time-sliced across frames instead of offloaded
private IEnumerator BuildGridSliced()
{
    for (int row = 0; row < _rows; row++)
    {
        BuildRow(row);
        yield return null;
    }
}
```

## What to use instead

| Need | Use |
|------|-----|
| Async I/O (network, asset load) | `async`/`await` on the main thread, `UnityWebRequest`, coroutines |
| Heavy CPU work | Time-slice across frames (coroutine / amortized loop — see frame-budget rule) |
| Delays / timers | `MainThreadDelay`, `Awaitable.WaitForSecondsAsync`, coroutines, `Time.time` comparisons |
| Parallel data crunch | C# Job System with Burst — jobs **run on the main thread** in WebGL but Burst still vectorizes; schedule and `Complete()` same frame, don't rely on overlap |

`async`/`await` itself works on WebGL (single-threaded `SynchronizationContext`) — continuations run on the main thread. That's fine; blocking on tasks and thread-pool timers are what kill you.

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No `Task.Delay` — use `MainThreadDelay` or `Awaitable.WaitForSecondsAsync`
- [ ] No `Thread`, `Task.Run`, `Parallel`, thread-pool, or timer APIs
- [ ] No `.Result` / `.Wait()` / `Thread.Sleep` blocking calls
- [ ] Heavy work time-sliced across frames, not "offloaded"
- [ ] No design that assumes background threads exist
